WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.770
Rust makes it easier to represent valid
states,

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.573
but the application still has to decide
what valid means.

3
00:00:05.653 --> 00:00:09.171
Input can arrive through HTTP requests,
files, command-line arguments,

4
00:00:09.251 --> 00:00:13.336
environment variables, queues, serialized
messages, databases, user interfaces,

5
00:00:13.416 --> 00:00:14.324
or other services.

6
00:00:14.404 --> 00:00:18.205
Until the application validates that
input, it should be treated as untrusted.

7
00:00:18.285 --> 00:00:22.257
Good Rust design uses explicit types,
parsing boundaries, validation functions,

8
00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:23.812
and clear rejection behavior.

9
00:00:23.892 --> 00:00:27.466
A string from outside the process should
not quietly become a trusted path,

10
00:00:27.546 --> 00:00:31.859
resource identifier, authorization
decision, query parameter, or downstream request.

11
00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:35.797
When possible, convert raw input into
domain-specific types after validation,

12
00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:39.054
so later code receives values that already
carry clearer meaning.

13
00:00:39.134 --> 00:00:43.276
Serialization and deserialization deserve
special attention because they often sit

14
00:00:43.356 --> 00:00:44.377
at trust boundaries.

15
00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:46.783
Data that crosses services, persists over
time,

16
00:00:46.863 --> 00:00:50.211
or comes from a queue may not match the
assumptions of the current code.

17
00:00:50.291 --> 00:00:52.674
Versioning, missing fields, unexpected
values,

18
00:00:52.754 --> 00:00:56.612
and overly permissive deserialization can
all create security and reliability

19
00:00:56.692 --> 00:00:58.394
problems even in memory-safe code.

20
00:00:58.474 --> 00:01:02.616
A secure Rust application avoids
scattering hidden assumptions across the codebase.

21
00:01:02.696 --> 00:01:06.100
It validates paths before file access,
resource IDs before selection,

22
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.904
request fields before authorization or
business logic,

23
00:01:08.984 --> 00:01:11.197
and outbound values before downstream
calls.

24
00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:13.660
Rejection should be clear, safe, and
consistent.

25
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.257
Invalid data should not produce vague
behavior, excessive error detail,

26
00:01:17.337 --> 00:01:19.380
or accidental fallback to a risky default.
